course: dictionary
lesson: 12863
title: Enjoining: ordering or urging
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Enjoin is a formal word. It means to order someone to do something, or to strongly urge them to do it. Enjoining is the -ing form, so it describes someone who is doing this right now. «The sign was enjoining visitors to keep off the grass.» The sign is not really talking, but it is giving a firm order: please do not walk on the grass.
explain: Look at the words around enjoining to find its meaning. The clue here is «to keep off the grass»: that is what the sign wants you to do. Ask yourself: what is being ordered or urged? Then try your meaning in the sentence. Does «the sign was strongly urging visitors to keep off the grass» still make sense? Yes, so you have it right.
explain: In law news, enjoining has a special meaning. A court enjoining someone is forbidding them to do something by an official order. «The court is enjoining the shop from selling fake toys.» The court has not just asked the shop nicely. It has made an official rule that the shop must stop.
fact: Enjoin means to order someone to do something, or to strongly urge them to.
fact: Enjoining is the -ing form of enjoin: enjoin, enjoins, enjoined, enjoining.
fact: In law, «a court enjoining» someone means the court officially forbids them to do something.
fact: Enjoining is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in reports, news about courts, and older or religious writing.
fact: Useful phrase: «enjoining someone to», as in «enjoining the team to listen carefully».
exercise: «The sign was enjoining visitors to keep off the grass.» What is the sign doing? | Painting the grass green | Measuring the grass | Telling a joke about grass | *Strongly urging visitors to keep off the grass
exercise: In a law news story: «The court is enjoining the shop from selling fake toys.» What does the court do? | Closes the road outside | *Officially forbids the shop from selling fake toys | Gives the shop a prize | Makes the shop sell more toys
exercise: Fill the gap with the best word: «Mum was ____ us to wash our hands before tea.» | laughing | forgetting | *enjoining | playing
test: «The rules are enjoining everyone to wear a helmet.» What does enjoining mean here? | Forgetting | Losing | Laughing | *Ordering or strongly urging
test: «The judge is ____ the company to stop the advert.» Choose the best word. | playing | *enjoining | forgetting | laughing
exam: «The notice was enjoining drivers to slow down near the school.» What is the notice doing? | Teaching drivers to sing | Asking drivers to buy a new car | Painting the school gates | *Strongly urging drivers to slow down
